From 5ff91afdea44a1d2433410fab9567c15f0add398 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 22:46:16 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 31/53] hydra: a colour landing on a scalar socket, and several
 helpers per mapping

MaterialX's opacity is a colour where the Cycles alpha it maps to is a single
number, and USD defines no cast between the two: the value fell through to the
generic conversion's warning and came back as zero. Every material that wrote
an opacity - white included, which is what Houdini's material builders author
by default - was rendered fully transparent, which reads as transmission
behaving like alpha. convertToCycles<float> now collapses a colour or a vector
to the average of its components, which protects every scalar socket rather
than just this one.

A mapping can now carry more than one helper node, each named, and an input
addressed to name:socket goes to that one. Fixed values learn to parse a vector
so a helper can carry a colour default. That lifts the limit which had kept
standard_surface's base unmapped: it is a weight on the diffuse colour and
Cycles has no socket for it, so the colour is multiplied on the way in, the
helper carrying MaterialX's own defaults for both.

Measured parameter by parameter against a reference render: 27 of the 39
standard_surface inputs are now honoured, and docs/STANDARD_SURFACE_AUDIT.md
says which of the remaining twelve have no Cycles equivalent and which would be
wrong if mapped.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
